.prawduct/project-state.yaml"], "findings": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "blocking", "title": "The NAS image's test target can no longer sync: arrt's dev group now needs ../contract, and the Dockerfile copies it in only after `uv sync --frozen`", "recommendation": "Inferred from reading, not measured: no build was run. `arrt/pyproject.toml` now puts `arrt-contract` in the dev group with `[tool.uv.sources] arrt-contract = { path = \"../contract\", editable = true }`, and `arrt/uv.lock` records it as `source = { editable = \"../contract\" }`. In `arrt/Dockerfile`'s `test` stage (WORKDIR /app), `RUN uv sync --frozen` installs the dev group, which means /app/../contract = /contract. But `COPY --from=repo contract /contract` comes two lines later. So the documented `docker buildx build --target test --build-context repo=. arrt/` (deploy/README.md \u00a7 The server on the NAS) fails at that sync, before any test runs. No CI job builds the image, so every suite stays green. Scope: instance. Move `COPY --from=repo contract /contract` above `RUN uv sync --frozen` in the test stage, and rewrite the comment there that says the tests look \"three levels above a test file\", since they now read the contract through `root()`. Then run the test-target build once to confirm the sync, and confirm the `--no-dev` app stage still builds without /contract present. Class: grep for other places that install arrt's dev group outside a full checkout (Dockerfiles, scripts in deploy/).", "files": ["arrt/pyproject.toml", "arrt/uv.lock"], "fid": "R-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "severity": "warning", "title": "The Player's copy of the deployment-values guard is narrower than the guard it replaces after 04", "recommendation": "The plan's architecture goal is \"Nothing that guards today guards less afterwards\", and its 03 decision says the Player \"carries its own copies scoped to itself\". The copy in `arrt-player/tests/test_repository.py` keeps the private-shape scan and the $HOME scan. It drops two checks that `tests/preferences/test_deployment_values.py` applies to the Player today. (1) `_this_machines_values`: the checkout's `.env` TV_ADDRESS, LATITUDE and LONGITUDE, checked across every tracked file, not only src/ and tools/. (2) `_DEPLOYMENT_PATHS` (`/srv/art`, `/opt/samsung-frame-art-loader`) in code. Nothing is lost while the root suite still walks arrt-player/. Once 04 extracts it, the public arrt-player repository has no check that its docs, artifacts or deploy files do not carry the wall's real address or coordinates. TV_ADDRESS is that repository's most sensitive value, and it already holds the files that name the Pi's paths. Scope: instance. Carry both checks into the Player's test_repository.py now, while the root guard can be compared against the copy (python-dotenv is already a Player runtime dependency). Each should fail once against a planted value.", "files": ["arrt-player/tests/test_repository.py", "tests/preferences/test_deployment_values.py"], "fid": "R-2"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Unintended", "severity": "note", "title": "The root project declares arrt-contract, but no root test imports it any more", "recommendation": "The root `pyproject.toml` dev group keeps `arrt-contract` and its `[tool.uv.sources]` path, with a comment saying \"the governance tests that check the planes against the contract (the heartbeat's two sides, the Player's routes) read it here\". After chunk 03, no module under tests/ imports `arrt_contract`. The heartbeat, staleness and plane-isolation tests moved to the planes, and `test_default_suite_ci_scope.py` only names the `contract/` directory. Scope: instance. Remove the dependency and its source entry, or keep it and change the comment to give the reason it stays. A reader following the comment will look for root tests that do not exist.", "files": ["pyproject.toml"], "fid": "R-3"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "warning", "title": "CLAUDE.md still says the root suite carries plane isolation, the heartbeat and the label corpus", "recommendation": "Instance. Chunk 03 moved these guards out of the root suite: plane isolation went to arrt-player/tests/test_plane_isolation.py, the heartbeat checks to arrt/tests/contract/test_heartbeat_key.py and arrt-player/tests/test_heartbeat_key.py, and the label-corpus check to arrt/tests/contract/test_label_corpus.py, arrt-player/tests/test_corpus_contract.py and contract/tests/test_label_corpus.py. CLAUDE.md lines 157-160 still list 'plane isolation, ... the heartbeat, ... the label corpus' as what tests/preferences/ carries. The table above that paragraph and the line 43 pointer were updated, so this paragraph now contradicts them. Consequence: a developer changing pull.py or the heartbeat key looks in, and runs, the root leg. That is the leg the paragraph says guards them, and it no longer does. Make the list relational (say the root suite holds only the checks that read more than one project, and name test_screen_tables.py as the example) instead of re-enumerating it. Line 69's 'Nothing in any of the three' also predates the fourth (contract) suite.", "files": ["CLAUDE.md"], "fid": "R-4"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "The root project depends on arrt-contract, but nothing in the root suite reads it", "recommendation": "Instance. pyproject.toml declares \"arrt-contract\" in the dev group, with a [tool.uv.sources] editable path to contract/. Its comment says the root tests 'that check the planes against the contract (the heartbeat's two sides, the Player's routes) read it here'. After chunk 03 no file under tests/ imports arrt_contract (git grep finds no hit), because both guards moved into the planes' suites. The dependency is now dead, and its comment points a maintainer at checks that no longer exist in this suite. Remove the dev entry and the uv source, and re-lock uv.lock. This also leaves the root with one fewer thing to unwind in chunk 05.", "files": ["pyproject.toml", "uv.lock"], "fid": "R-5"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "Three diverging copies of the project-derivation helper in the root suite", "recommendation": "Class: the `git ls-files '*pyproject.toml'` derivation chunk 03 introduced. There are three copies: tests/test_repo_hygiene.py::_projects, tests/preferences/test_deployment_values.py::_projects and tests/preferences/test_file_organization.py::_projects. They have already diverged. test_file_organization's copy lacks the 'no project's pyproject.toml is tracked' vacuity assert that the other two treat as essential, so an empty listing leaves _TEST_TREES as just ('tests/',) and the placement check passes silently. test_repo_hygiene's copy runs git with no cwd at import time and checks Path(project, 'tools').is_dir() relative to the process cwd, while the other two anchor to the repository root. This derivation is the mechanism that keeps these scans honest when arrt-player leaves at chunk 05, so one owner matters. Fix: one helper (tests/preferences already hosts a shared import_graph.py), anchored to the repository root and carrying the vacuity assert, with all three suites going through it.", "files": ["tests/test_repo_hygiene.py", "tests/preferences/test_deployment_values.py", "tests/preferences/test_file_organization.py"], "fid": "R-6"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "note", "title": "corpus.py's 'Copied rather than imported' paragraph now runs two seams together", "recommendation": "Instance; prose, so nothing reads it. The rewritten sentence gives two reasons for the copy in one clause. One is that the preview tool runs on a Player without the contract package; the other is that an import 'across that seam' would be 'the one place the two planes touched'. The second reason is about the curation plane, not the contract, so it no longer explains why the records are copied from vectors/label-corpus.json. If touched, delete the curation-seam clause. The remaining reason (no contract package at runtime) is the one the test docstring in tests/test_corpus_contract.py gives. No edit is owed.", "files": ["arrt-player/src/arrt_player/panel/corpus.py"], "fid": "R-7"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "note", "title": "Vector files are read by raw path and shape, unlike the package's other data", "recommendation": "Class, bounded by `git grep 'root() / \"vectors\"'`, which finds five sites today. The package wraps routes(), fixtures() and timing(). The vectors, including label-corpus.json, which chunk 03 added, are opened by each consumer with its own path join, and the label corpus's [\"records\"] shape is spelled in three suites: contract/tests/test_label_corpus.py, arrt/tests/contract/test_label_corpus.py and arrt-player/tests/test_corpus_contract.py. The design deliberately exposes root() plus the paths the specification names, so this is a consistency question, not a defect. A vector(name) accessor would put the file's shape behind the package before the Player reads it from a tag in another repo, where a reshape needs a coordinated release. Accept or defer.", "files": ["contract/src/arrt_contract/__init__.py", "arrt-player/tests/test_corpus_contract.py", "arrt/tests/contract/test_label_corpus.py", "contract/tests/test_label_corpus.py"], "fid": "R-8"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "warning", "title": "Owner goal 2 ('a published package') is built and about to be tagged on an inference the owner has not ratified", "recommendation": "Instance. The plan's Goals record the owner's answer as 'a published package', and the Open assumptions still mark its meaning as inferred at MED impact: an annotated git tag `contract-v<version>` on brookstalley/arrt, not PyPI. This PR builds that reading into the shipped surface (contract/player-contract.md \u00a7 The package names the tag and the git+https install line; contract/pyproject.toml and every plane's pyproject comment say 'a Player outside this repository takes a released tag'), and chunk 03's Done-when pushes `contract-v1.0.0` at the merge commit, after which chunk 04 pins the Player to it. The consequence: a tag is meant to be immutable, so if the owner meant PyPI, the first released version and the Player's first lockfile are both built on the wrong channel. The second MED inference (which artifacts leave with the Player) is consumed at 04, so it can wait, but name it in the same question. Put the tag-vs-PyPI reading to the owner before the tag is pushed and record the answer in the plan's Goals in place of the 'Inferred' line; no code change is needed if the answer is 'tag'.", "files": ["contract/player-contract.md", "contract/pyproject.toml"], "fid": "R-9"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "note", "title": "Mid-build decisions for the owner, including one departure from the owner's 2026-10-05 ruling still awaiting a veto-or-accept", "recommendation": "Scope: none. Listing for the owner what the plan settled mid-build and what settled it. 01: data stays at contract/schemas|fixtures|vectors|routes.json with the wheel force-including it ('do it right': 58 citing files); player-contract.md travels via symlink + sdist real path (wave 5 row); the Player does not import semantic_errors (an error in the plan's Done-when 3, corrected; this is the one alignment-pass miss in 01); package tests are the three moved root files plus test_package.py; post-review: every caller reads through validator()/routes()/schema(). 02: the hand-run tools read arrt_player.config.load() instead of carrying the root's config.py, which DEPARTS from the letter of the owner's 2026-10-05 ruling ('and the config.py they read') and is recorded as a builder DECISION 'user can veto', explicitly flagged for this boundary review, so the owner should see it before merge; all.json and deploy/pi-freeze-2024.txt stay (the plan's removal list was wrong about both: a second alignment miss); remove_from_tv/forgettable_ids/describe_removal and ten tests left with tvart.py; tests/test_config.py split into tests/preferences/test_deployment_values.py; UPLOADED_CATEGORY now comes from arrt_player.tv.client, with the rewire onto SamsungTv.remove filed as #384. 03: cross-plane agreements restated against the contract (instant_key, timing.json, vectors/label-corpus.json); the anonymous-record title gap is a named exception pending #385; plane isolation moved whole into the Player suite with a copied import reader; the Player's deploy files and their root guards stay until 04/05 so the operator's procedure doesn't change mid-wave.", "files": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-wave-5-split.md"], "fid": "R-10"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "note", "title": "scope-trace: arrt_contract.specification() is consumed only by its own test", "recommendation": "Instance, and ambiguous, so NOTE. specification() traces to the wave 5 row's requirement to pin the contract 'together with player-contract.md', but the only caller in the tree is contract/tests/test_package.py; no suite, tool or plan step (chunk 04 included) reads the released specification through it. Its consumer is presumably a human or agent in the future arrt-player repo reading the pinned text. If that's the intent, a line in chunk 04 naming who reads it (for example, the Player's CLAUDE.md pointing at the pinned spec) closes the trace; otherwise the wheel carrying the file is enough and the accessor can go.", "files": ["contract/src/arrt_contract/__init__.py"], "fid": "R-11"}, {"goal": "Learnings Cross-Check", "severity": "warning", "title": "A pending operator walk still instructs `pip install -r requirements.txt` and a root `tv_api_check.py` after both were retired", "recommendation": "Class, bounded. Escalated from the cross-check's NOTE because the change reintroduces the pattern core.md warns against: 'When you retire, amend, descope or close anything, first grep the whole repo (no --include) for its nouns and OLD wording'